 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080022 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080022 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "93fd8ea2-7862-53ca-acaa-f7e54190b630" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080023 #edb/ref 1080 "images/a1/a1b0d99c42318245a2e61ae122aabe3e6f30642ba8589c1a4e8d14921d1653a0.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080023 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080023 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "99eb8012-1b96-5de5-8d46-cdab89bb819c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080024 #edb/ref 1080 "images/c8/c8d497a6867f41010998bb7504ae7c9ec34534582e0a41a30b7008a326090416.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080024 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080024 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a119e827-41ce-5cab-b2db-d70a3daba859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080025 #edb/ref 1080 "images/ce/ceb21f25a7484607d1f1015f0d3eece084b9502d986c1615f84d174e772514d8.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080025 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080025 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d01238a9-9418-52ac-ad4c-cd558bd0cc58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080026 #edb/ref 1080 "images/da/dac12a81adb4559e93171245f3be9a55520017a46218f4945e85044ed0ac4d61.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080026 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186080026 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "27769851-5197-5135-98b5-c57d00160af1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083139 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083139 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/aa/aa9f4ce130811186b476064a3d0b828daf9f5ded3d9011eda832f3bc470f8334.png)\n\nThe scatterplot above shows the correspondence between the mean daily temperature outside a particular coffee shop and the number of customers ordering hot drinks. Which of the following best describes the correlation between the variables in the scatter plot?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083139 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "87541606-5e53-5e3c-aefa-b3d6a6a12961" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083139 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/b9/b970e539af5b695644486ad4eb7c21558c334ffd33c1df1a2343fac493d84389.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, we have a *negative* correlation.\n\n- The points are scattered quite far from the corresponding trend line. So, we have a *weak* correlation.\n\nTherefore, the variables have a *weak negative* linear correlation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083140 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083140 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4e/4e3c58da0dc7f326497d9a7c1327d6d40f98684089694f9de88f21a64f4b4824.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the correlation coefficient for the data in the scatter plot above?\n\nI. $\\rho =-0.8$\n\nII. $\\rho= 0.3$\n\nIII. $\\rho = 0.9$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083140 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8aaa2e17-cd20-5d59-80c5-6ec3c7842672" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083140 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho = 1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho = -1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/06/06dcc617bbbc07fea756f35975146a238bd4cb9c4ce437f1b9300a7c05ded7b6.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *positive* slope. So, the correlation coefficient must be *positive*. Thus, we disregard the following value:\n\n$$\n\\rho = -0.8\n$$\n\n- The points lie pretty close to the corresponding trend line. So, the correlation coefficient must be closer to $1.$ Thus, we disregard the following value:\n\n$$\n\\rho = 0.3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient from the given options is $\\rho= 0.9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083141 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9698" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083141 #edb/ref 1071 "It is known that there is a linear correlation between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Given that the correlation coefficient is $\\rho\\approx -0.85,$ sketch a possible scatter plot that corresponds to this situation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083141 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e64e6c3-80f5-5b23-90fa-8512ad377948" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083141 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho = 1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho = -1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\approx -0.85,$ there is a *strong negative* linear correlation in the data.\n\n- The *negative* linear correlation means that the data points should be scattered along a line with a *negative* slope.\n\n- Since the correlation is *strong*, most of the points should lie *close* to the trend line.\n\nTherefore, this situation could correspond to the following scatter plot:\n\n![](images/ad/ad8ff5e49649b5e788b83eb28fa26afae61072d5e63026631a9cd1891be14a0c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083337 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083337 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083337 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083337 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083337 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083337 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083337 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Type and Strength of a Given Correlation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083337 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186083139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083337 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ef670158-f161-51f8-b146-0c07f8a1fa5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083338 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083338 #edb/ref 1039 "Estimating the Correlation Coefficient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083338 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186083140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083338 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b973aca4-f9fc-5b5c-ab75-2329204993ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083339 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying a Scatter Plot Given a Correlation Coefficient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083339 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186083141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083339 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ebbc6b77-cd04-5509-b344-66feddfed1bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083427 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083427 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083427 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083427 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083427 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083427 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083427 #edb/ref 1045 "Linear Correlation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083427 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186084128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083427 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083427 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083427 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "379bb0b9-1655-5b9e-836d-497f51622d41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083516 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186079991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083516 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133916" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083516 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083516 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/33/3397b66cdd256455a242eef55f48d59881662036f109f17d24ea9d0836097d75.png)\n\nA company produces cars. The scatter plot above shows the correspondence between the number of cars produced each week and the company's profit, in thousands of dollars. Which of the following best describes the correlation between the variables in the scatter plot?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083516 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dae8b0b0-26c1-5e76-af20-c4931e835d1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083516 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/6b/6b21527790fddecf89cd72a2e1d1f67338d21d82c1f3090536be973fcd60919a.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *positive* slope. So, we have a *positive* correlation.\n\n- The points lie pretty close to the corresponding trend line. So, we have a *strong* correlation.\n\nTherefore, the variables have a *strong positive* linear correlation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083517 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186079997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083517 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133917" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083517 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083517 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/72/72315da1e53905325845f0152026f209796d490328fca115ea468edfa0b33d78.png)\n\nWhich of the following best describes the correlation between the variables in the scatter plot above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083517 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ab5e4f1-1334-5d5b-870a-2fdfdb0151c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083517 #edb/ref 1074 "From the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear correlation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083518 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083518 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083518 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083518 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/04/0432f45ba5b0652edc7f620233e9d2095c6e6836e98cecddf9ebc6f8c4cf99a5.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the best approximation of the correlation coefficient for the data in the scatter plot above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083518 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3e1cea1-0dea-5f3c-a617-a3cfcf6a3eac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083518 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/91/91d605a297f2fd3c90c59592cc08184c4201f04ebc69bfc285e4261af8e918d2.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, the correlation coefficient must be *negative*. Thus, we disregard the following values:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈0.9,\\,\\rho ≈0.1,\\,\\rho ≈0\n$$\n\n- The points lie pretty close to the corresponding trend line. So, the correlation coefficient must be closer to $-1.$ Thus, we disregard the following value:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈-0.2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient must be $\\rho ≈-0.8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083521 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083521 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083521 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083521 #edb/ref 1071 "It is known that there is a linear correlation between the number of years passed since $2000$ and the population of a particular town. Given that the correlation coefficient is $\\rho ≈0.9,$ which of the following scatter plots corresponds to this situation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083521 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dce7c2f3-5685-5680-ac64-38c6bb59f9b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083521 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nTherefore, since $\\rho ≈0.9,$ there is a *strong positive* linear correlation in the data.\n\n- The *positive* linear correlation means that the data points should be scattered along a line with a *positive* slope.\n\n- Since the correlation is *strong*, most of the points should lie *close* to the trend line.\n\nTherefore, this situation corresponds to the following scatter plot.\n\n![](images/aa/aac79b793bde599899ff7ff8ca880e198bd5481178c2c5a0e2ac96acf223bdaa.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084128 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084128 #edb/ref 1061 20618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084128 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "95a44919-d4ba-5d32-a2bd-f69011dce161" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084128 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084290 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084290 #edb/ref 1061 36348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084290 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2da7dcd7-da21-537a-8c2e-c0b52615035c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084290 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084291 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084291 #edb/ref 1061 36349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084291 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f826ecdd-fef8-5bd8-90d8-82a4bd2eac1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084291 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084292 #edb/ref 1061 36350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084292 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "93e678b9-6925-580f-85e0-aaf4018c8a15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084292 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084293 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084293 #edb/ref 1061 36361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084293 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "40606d75-7f4e-5175-b3e7-b96e62b5b149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084293 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084452 #edb/ref 1064 "There is a **linear correlation** between two sets of paired observations if the data points on the corresponding scatter plot are located along a straight line.\n\nWe say that the correlation is **positive** if the corresponding trend line has a *positive slope*. Similarly, we say that the correlation is **negative** if the corresponding trend line has a *negative slope*.\n\nWe have a **strong** correlation if the data points lie *close* to the corresponding trend line. Similarly, we have a **weak** correlation if the data points are scattered *far* from the corresponding trend line.\n\nLet's see a few examples:\n\n| ![](images/cd/cdd335fda9e91cbf7cdb05ad18065630985c50adb788df980708d822f7a5e8e9.png) | **Strong positive** linear correlation:\n\n- The trend line has a *positive* slope.\n\n- The data points lie *close* to the trend line. |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![](images/9b/9b288c25fb0993a714768e3e2e9caf23a11f2629950eb03b138acfc0aa6c17cf.png) | **Weak positive** linear correlation: \n\n- The trend line has a *positive* slope.\n\n- The data points lie *far* from the trend line. |\n| ![](images/36/3680700a5e4e4e196db6ffe106535ad784567b9fbed85e5005cbb6e49a476860.png) | **Strong negative** linear correlation:\n\n- The trend line has a *negative* slope.\n\n- The data points lie *close* to the trend line. |\n| ![](images/07/076440b257a78474e9f1b3129ec448a407bb1b49215a29251d8e929cadaeb984.png) | **Weak negative** linear correlation:\n\n- The trend line has a *negative* slope.\n\n- The data points lie *far* from the trend line. |\n| ![](images/ed/ed266d09a86d7200c02f004cd9bb3a87f75693462ff8846f7f3cc1ed4b66ed63.png) | **No linear correlation**:\n\n- The data points are scattered randomly. |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084452 #edb/ref 1065 3698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084452 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4d19b15d-f5c7-53da-b834-26b49ff2c356" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084452 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084490 #edb/ref 1064 "The linear **correlation coefficient** $\\rho$ (the Greek letter rho, pronounced \"row\") is a real number between $-1$ and $1$ that shows the strength of a linear correlation. It has the following properties:\n\n- $\\rho = 1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation\n\n- $\\rho = -1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation\n\n- $\\rho = 0$ indicates no (linear) correlation\n\nThe approximate value of $\\rho$ can be determined from the scatter plot.\n\n| ![](images/09/09d585da995df4e5a67b022816eec246113171d3aad15dcb57d82c766dcb7d18.png) | For a strong positive correlation, we have $0.7 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1.$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![](images/9b/9b288c25fb0993a714768e3e2e9caf23a11f2629950eb03b138acfc0aa6c17cf.png) | For a weak positive correlation, we have $0.3 \\leq \\rho \\lt 0.7.$ |\n| ![](images/36/3680700a5e4e4e196db6ffe106535ad784567b9fbed85e5005cbb6e49a476860.png) | For a strong negative correlation, we have $-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq -0.7.$ |\n| ![](images/92/92b65a115fd1621303a248de8ae03d05392827914f5200f267d368867d511ae5.png) | For a weak negative correlation, we have $-0.7 \\lt \\rho \\leq -0.3.$ |\n| ![](images/2c/2cf1e2c3e79940229fc85c10d5bccdfed17afe0759cc897d53e5421328b21466.png) | For little or no correlation, we have $-0.3 < \\rho < 0.3.$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084490 #edb/ref 1065 5951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084490 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c84adf99-1723-5b00-9eb9-b729a194dbe3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084490 #edb/ref 1067 "The Correlation Coefficient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545202 #edb/ref 1080 "\\rho ≈0.4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545202 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545202 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01a7c6d4-0487-53da-a22e-734216f21437" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545206 #edb/ref 1080 "images/88/8878fd97d3c1ebb87b849bfb1da28b03821dc1f11fecf26fb98386fcbf8e1d83.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545206 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545206 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03b5013b-1b8d-5ea5-acf9-9e6a17e86485" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545210 #edb/ref 1080 "strong positive linear correlation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545210 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545210 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04c24679-f692-5bfb-9d7c-05f5dc96b029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545212 #edb/ref 1080 "strong negative linear correlation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545212 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545212 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0660e208-70e5-5b04-ad6a-42881363a6ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545221 #edb/ref 1080 "\\rho ≈0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545221 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545221 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c7cb220-5afc-5b19-a7bf-aeaaba188e81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545248 #edb/ref 1080 "\\rho ≈0.4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545248 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545248 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "18de5bcf-ee53-583d-9955-d6382d059b1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545251 #edb/re